## Development

```bash
uv sync
uv run {{ cli_name }} --help
uv run pytest --snapshot-update  # after changing a command's help; review tests/snapshots/help/
```

`pytest` fails below the coverage floor in `pyproject.toml`; `ruff` includes
the security (`S`) rules; the gate also scans the git history for committed
secrets when `gitleaks` is installed (`brew install gitleaks`) and `uv.lock`
for known vulnerabilities when `osv-scanner` is installed (`brew install
osv-scanner`), and says it skipped a scan otherwise. The gate is local and it
is the only gate: run the `[publish] gate_command` of
`.agents/antagor/antagor.toml` (`gate check`) before every commit.

## Release

The version is the git tag (`vX.Y.Z`) — `pyproject.toml` has no `version =`.
Work and releases go through Antagor.

## Install

**On this machine, from a local checkout:**

```bash
uv tool install .
```

**From a release asset — no index, no build, no git** (the release workflow
attaches the wheel and the sdist to every `vX.Y.Z` tag):

```bash
uv tool install "{{ dist_name }} @ {% if repository_url and repository_url.startswith('https://github.com/') %}https://github.com/{{ repository_url | replace('https://github.com/', '') }}/releases/download/vX.Y.Z/<wheel-file>.whl{% else %}https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/releases/download/vX.Y.Z/<wheel-file>.whl{% endif %}"
```

**Or from the git tag** (needs git; never install a branch):

```bash
{% if repository_url -%}
uv tool install "{{ dist_name }} @ git+{{ repository_url }}.git@vX.Y.Z"
{%- else -%}
uv tool install "{{ dist_name }} @ git+https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>.git@vX.Y.Z"
{%- endif %}
```

**In a container, from a wheelhouse** — reproducible, and the image needs no
network at build time:

```dockerfile
COPY dist/*.whl /wheelhouse/
RUN uv pip install --no-index --find-links /wheelhouse {{ dist_name }}
```

No package-index address appears in this package: an index, if the deployment
has one, is configured by the environment (`UV_INDEX`), never by the package.

Replace `vX.Y.Z` with the latest release tag. This is also how an AI agent
bootstraps this CLI on a fresh machine/sandbox where it isn't installed
yet{% if include_skill %} — see `src/{{ package_name }}/data/SKILL.md`{% endif %}.

**Private repo?** The asset and git forms need the same credentials as
`git clone`. On a machine where the install is refused with an auth error, do
one of these once:

```bash
gh auth login          # interactive login also sets up git's credential helper
gh auth setup-git      # only needed if gh was logged in with --with-token / in CI
```

or use the SSH form of the git install:

```bash
{% if repository_url -%}
uv tool install "{{ dist_name }} @ git+ssh://git@github.com/{{ repository_url | replace('https://github.com/', '') }}.git@vX.Y.Z"
{%- else -%}
uv tool install "{{ dist_name }} @ git+ssh://git@github.com/<owner>/<repo>.git@vX.Y.Z"
{%- endif %}
```

**Update** an installed copy to the latest commit:

```bash
uv tool upgrade {{ dist_name }}
```

After install, the command `{{ cli_name }}` is available globally, on
macOS, Linux and Windows alike (no shelling out, no hardcoded POSIX paths).

To pick up later architecture changes from the `cli-generator` template
itself, run `copier update` in this repo (requires this repo and the
template to both be git repos).

## Architecture

This project follows a fixed CLI architecture:

- `src/{{ package_name }}/commands/` — argument parsing only, no business logic.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/core/` — the real logic and the errors.
{%- if include_providers %}
- `src/{{ package_name }}/providers/` — external APIs/integrations.
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_auth %}
- `src/{{ package_name }}/credentials/` — secrets via the OS keychain (`keyring`)
  and environment variables. Secrets never live in a project file.
{%- endif %}
- `src/{{ package_name }}/output/` — human (`rich`) and `--json` rendering.

Run `{{ cli_name }} doctor` to check what the CLI needs to run, and
`{{ cli_name }} --help` for the full command surface. Every command supports
`--json` for machine/agent consumption; errors are structured (`error.code`,
fixed exit codes, `error.action` telling you what to do next) and go to
stderr in human mode, so stdout is always safe to pipe.

{%- if include_project %}

A project can require a minimum CLI version in its `.{{ package_name }}/config.toml`
(`[cli] min_version = "..."`); an older install refuses with exit 6 and
`uv tool upgrade {{ dist_name }}` fixes it.
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_skill %}

